The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
static unsigned long sp804_read(void)
{
	return ~readl_relaxed(sched_clkevt->value);
}
...
static void sp804_register_delay_timer(int freq)
{
	delay.freq = freq;
	delay.read_current_timer = sp804_read;
	register_current_timer_delay(&delay);
}
...
sp804_register_delay_timer(rate);   // called before sched_clkevt is assigned
```
Reason:
The delay timer’s `read_current_timer` callback was set to `sp804_read`, which reads from `sched_clkevt->value`. However, `sched_clkevt` is only assigned when `use_sched_clock` is true (inside the `if (use_sched_clock)` block). When `use_sched_clock` is false, `sched_clkevt` remains uninitialized or NULL. Since `sp804_register_delay_timer(rate)` is called unconditionally before any assignment to `sched_clkevt`, any subsequent call to `read_current_timer` (via the delay timer) dereferences an invalid pointer, causing an Oops.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
static struct sp804_clkevt *delay_clkevt;
static unsigned long sp804_read_delay_timer_read(void)
{
	return ~readl_relaxed(delay_clkevt->value);
}
static void sp804_register_delay_timer(struct sp804_clkevt *clk, int freq)
{
	delay_clkevt = clk;
	delay.freq = freq;
	delay.read_current_timer = sp804_read_delay_timer_read;
	register_current_timer_delay(&delay);
}
...
clkevt = sp804_clkevt_get(base);
// ... initialization of clkevt ...
sp804_register_delay_timer(clkevt, rate);   // now called after clkevt is ready
```
Reason:
A dedicated global pointer `delay_clkevt` is introduced to hold the clock event instance used exclusively for the delay timer. The `sp804_read_delay_timer_read` function reads from this dedicated pointer instead of the shared `sched_clkevt`. The `sp804_register_delay_timer` now takes a `struct sp804_clkevt *` parameter, so the caller passes the valid `clkevt` obtained after initialization. This decouples the delay timer from the sched_clock, ensuring `read_current_timer` works correctly regardless of whether `use_sched_clock` is set.

